+# Changes
+
+## Round 0
+
+round 0: no traces yet; hardened from the contract.
+
+### src/csv.mjs
+
+Rewrote `parseCsvLine` as a single-pass, index-based state machine
+(`FIELD_START` / `UNQUOTED` / `QUOTED` / `AFTER_QUOTE`) that enforces every
+line of the README contract. The previous version toggled a `quoted` flag on
+every `"` and never reported an error, so it accepted `a"b`, `"a"b`, unclosed
+quotes, stray line breaks, and turned `""` into nothing instead of `"`.
+
+What is now enforced:
+
+- Non-string input (including `String` objects, Buffers, objects with
+  `toString`) throws `TypeError`; every string input returns `string[]` or
+  throws `CsvError`. There is no other code path that can throw: no recursion,
+  no regex, no `JSON`, only `charCodeAt` and `slice` in a bounded `for` loop.
+- `,` separates fields; empty text is `[""]`; trailing comma yields a trailing
+  empty field.
+- Quoted fields: `""` inside quotes is one `"`; commas, `\n`, `\r\n` (and any
+  other character) inside quotes are literal.
+- After a closing quote only `,`, the trailing record terminator, or end of
+  text may follow; anything else is a `CsvError`. An unclosed quote is a
+  `CsvError`.
+- A `"` outside a quoted field (anywhere but the first character of a field)
+  is a `CsvError`.
+- Whitespace outside quotes is kept verbatim.
+- Exactly one `\n` or `\r\n` at the very end is stripped before parsing; any
+  other unquoted `\r` or `\n` (including a lone trailing `\r`, `\n\n`,
+  `\r\n\r\n`, `\n\r`) is a `CsvError`.
+- Every other code unit (tabs, NUL, C0/C1 controls, BOM, lone surrogates,
+  Unicode quote/comma look-alikes) is literal.
+- Performance is O(n) in time and memory. Unquoted fields and runs inside
+  quoted fields are copied with `slice` rather than per-character
+  concatenation. 100 KB parses in ~1 ms; 10 MB adversarial inputs (all
+  commas, all escaped quotes, all unbalanced quotes) parse in under 200 ms.
+
+`CsvError` now also carries an `index` property (code-unit offset of the
+failure) and a descriptive message; it remains an `Error` subclass with
+`name === "CsvError"`.
+
+### tests/csv.test.mjs
+
+Extended from 4 to 20 tests, one block per README contract line, plus:
+
+- a set of 100 KB (and 1 MB) records in every adversarial shape, each asserted
+  to finish in under one second and to produce only `string[]` or `CsvError`;
+- a 20 000-case seeded pseudo-random sweep and an exhaustive sweep of all
+  strings up to length 6 over the alphabet `" , \n \r a space`, both
+  cross-checked against an independent reference implementation of the README
+  and asserting that nothing other than `CsvError` is ever thrown.
+
+### Traces fixed this round
+
+None (no traces/ directory exists yet).
+
+## Round 1
+
+### Traces fixed this round
+
+- `traces/range-error-on-many-fields.md` — `",".repeat(2 ** 27)` (128 MiB of
+  commas, 134 217 729 fields) threw V8's `RangeError: Invalid array length`
+  from `fields.push` instead of a `CsvError`. Now throws
+  `CsvError: Too many fields at position 134217727; a record may have at most
+  134217728 fields`. Verified by running the trace's exact command (node
+  v25.2.1, macOS arm64, default heap): ~1.8 s, ~2.4 GB RSS. Also verified:
+  `",".repeat(2 ** 27 - 1)` still returns the 134 217 728-element array,
+  `",".repeat(209715200)` (the trace's 200 MB variant) throws `CsvError`, and
+  `'"a",'.repeat(52428800)` (200 MB, 52 428 801 fields) still parses.
+
+### Root cause
+
+The result is one JS array and V8 cannot grow an array past 2^27 =
+134 217 728 elements via `push`, whatever the heap size (probed directly:
+push number 134 217 729 throws `RangeError`). So a string with more than
+2^27 fields cannot be returned as `string[]` at all; the only
+contract-compliant outcome is a `CsvError`. The parser had no bound of its
+own and let the engine's error escape.
+
+### src/csv.mjs
+
+- New exported constant `MAX_FIELDS = 2 ** 27`: the maximum number of fields
+  a record may have. The parser checks `fields.length` right after each
+  comma's push and throws `CsvError("Too many fields at position i …", i)`
+  at the comma that starts the excess field. This is the earliest point the
+  overflow is certain (a comma after `maxFields` complete fields means one
+  more field regardless of what follows), the array never grows past the
+  bound, and the final push at end of text needs no check. The bound is a
+  general rule over the field count, not a match on the trace input: it
+  fires for commas, unquoted and quoted fields alike, and is exercised by
+  the test suite through a small injected bound.
+- New exported `createCsvLineParser({ maxFields })` that returns a parser
+  with a different bound (validated: positive safe integer, else
+  `TypeError`). This is how the tests reach the overflow path with tiny
+  inputs. `parseCsvLine(text)` is unchanged in signature and equals
+  `createCsvLineParser()` behaviour; a factory was chosen over an optional
+  second parameter so that `lines.map(parseCsvLine)` cannot silently pass an
+  index as the bound.
+- The parser proper moved into an internal `parseRecord(text, maxFields)`;
+  `parseCsvLine` wraps it in a last-resort guard that converts any
+  non-`CsvError` exception into a `CsvError` (with `cause` set to the
+  original). `parseRecord` has no code path of its own that throws anything
+  else, so this guard is only reachable through an engine resource limit
+  below `MAX_FIELDS` on some other engine/build. It is deliberately not
+  provoked in the suite (it would need a real engine limit); only the shape
+  of the error it builds is tested. `CsvError` therefore gained an optional
+  third `cause` argument.
+- Performance is unchanged: one integer compare per field. The 100 KB and
+  1 MB timing tests report the same numbers as before (~13 ms / ~23 ms for
+  the whole batch).
+
+### tests/csv.test.mjs
+
+Four tests added (20 -> 24, all passing):
+
+- `MAX_FIELDS is the engine's array limit and is what parseCsvLine uses`:
+  pins `MAX_FIELDS === 2 ** 27` and checks the boundary arithmetic of the
+  trace (`n - 1` commas parse to `n` fields, `n` commas throw) on an injected
+  bound of 2^4.
+- `too many fields is a CsvError, not a RangeError (trace:
+  range-error-on-many-fields)`: with `maxFields: 3`, every shape of a
+  fourth field (only commas — the trace's shape —, unquoted, quoted,
+  trailing empty, with terminator, far past the bound, 10 000 commas) throws
+  a `CsvError` that is not a `RangeError`, with `index` pointing at the
+  offending comma; records with at most three fields parse as before;
+  ordering against other syntax errors; `maxFields: 1` still admits every
+  single-field record in the README.
+- `createCsvLineParser defaults to MAX_FIELDS and rejects bad bounds`.
+- `CsvError can carry a cause without an index`.
+
+Why not reproduce the trace verbatim in the suite: the input is 128 MiB and
+the run needs ~2.4 GB of RSS and ~2 s, which would dominate the suite and is
+brittle on CI memory limits. The overflow path is a single condition
+(`fields.length === maxFields`) at the three comma sites; driving it through
+`createCsvLineParser({ maxFields: 3 })` executes exactly that code with the
+same arithmetic and no special handling of the trace's input, so the
+regression is covered deterministically in well under a millisecond. The
+128 MiB run was done by hand and is recorded above.
