+# Trace: with `Error.prototype` frozen, every syntax error escapes as `TypeError`
+
+## README line violated
+
+> For every string `text`, `parseCsvLine` either returns the fields as an array
+> of strings, or throws `CsvError` (exported, a subclass of `Error`). It never
+> throws anything else, ...
+
+## Preconditions
+
+`Error.prototype` is frozen before the call. This is what hardened realms do
+(SES `lockdown()`, `harden`, or a plain `Object.freeze(Error.prototype)` in an
+app's bootstrap); the parser is imported into such an environment unchanged.
+No other tampering: `Array.prototype`, `String.prototype`, `Object.prototype`
+untouched. Also reproduces if only `Error.prototype.name` is made
+non-writable.
+
+## Input
+
+Any string the parser must reject, e.g. `'a"b'` (README: "Outside quotes, a
+`"` anywhere is a `CsvError`"). Valid records are unaffected (`'a,b'` still
+returns `["a", "b"]`).
+
+## Command (from the project root)
+
+```sh
+node --input-type=module -e 'import { parseCsvLine, CsvError } from "./src/csv.mjs"; Object.freeze(Error.prototype); try { console.log(parseCsvLine(String.fromCharCode(97, 34, 98))); } catch (e) { console.log("threw:", e.constructor.name, "| instanceof CsvError:", e instanceof CsvError, "|", e.message); }'
+```
+
+(`String.fromCharCode(97, 34, 98)` is `a"b`; it only avoids quoting inside
+`-e`.) Node v25.2.1, default flags.
+
+## Expected
+
+```
+threw: CsvError | instanceof CsvError: true | Unexpected quote at position 1; ...
+```
+
+## Actual
+
+```
+threw: TypeError | instanceof CsvError: false | Cannot assign to read only property 'name' of object 'Error: Record could not be parsed: Cannot assign to read only property 'name' of object 'Error: Unexpected quote at position 1; a quote may only appear inside a quoted field''
+```
+
+## Cause
+
+`CsvError`'s constructor does `this.name = "CsvError"`. Once `Error.prototype`
+is frozen, `name` is an inherited non-writable data property, and strict-mode
+assignment (ESM is strict) throws `TypeError` inside `new CsvError(...)`. The
+guard in `parseChecked` catches that `TypeError` and tries to build another
+`CsvError`, which fails the same way, so the nested `TypeError` is what the
+caller sees. The same happens for every other `CsvError` path (unclosed quote,
+stray `\r`, too many fields, the resource-limit wrapper).
+
+Defining `name` on the prototype (or with `Object.defineProperty(this, "name",
+...)`) instead of assigning it avoids the write to an inherited read-only slot.
